e can't always be at  a ten outta ten hype it needs to build  up to that if we are constantly pushing  the ceiling up and up and up all the  time then eventually we become  desensitized to what is at stake that is  if we haven't already  my point is the stakes our scope of a  particular story only matters as much as  the emotional resonance we have for the  characters involved for instance if Tony  created a villain that would instantly  erase all of Goku's friends that would  be just as emotionally devastating as  someone destroying the universe simply  put one death is a tragedy one million  deaths is a statistic when the stakes  become absurdly high it starts to become  increasingly more difficult to  appreciate the scope of the consequences  simply throwing more at the situation  isn't good storytelling so this galactic  patrol Ark decided to focus on the  internal conflict of the namekian people  and Vegeta I greatly appreciate it  because now I have something to root for  I genuinely enjoy the notion  Vegeta feels tremendous remorse for his  past actions on Namek resulting in his  character feeling compelled to go above  and beyond for these people in danger  this is a wonderful fresh and believable  story for Vegeta to partaken and I'm  here for it I've also seen people upset  that the namekians Savior or fuse  namekian warrior was taken down so  easily by Mauro personally I don't see  this as an issue in the slightest in the  tournament of power such beings already  existed in universe 6  additionally in chapter 45 they already  had a large portion of their energy  stolen to suggest Mauro is capable of  what he did against the namekian warrior  isn't really a tough pill to swallow  when you consider that even when he was  unhealthy he was still going toe to toe  against a Super Saiyan God and not even  all the namekians were involved in that  fusion in fact know that I think about  it I'd go as far as to say that if they  did stand a chance against him it would  be incredibly inconsistent however that  is just a minor gripe people have had  now on to bigger criticisms another big  gripe people seem to have with the story  is miracies ability to outmaneuver a  Super Saiyan 3 Goku this leads to a host  of questions about his abilities and how  they formed which as of writing this  video has not been explained I have  several questions  naturally this leads all of us to think  surely he could have helped with Frieza  or sell or Buu it is established in  chapter 43 that he's not hiding his  power but is incredibly agile and quick  this was while Goku and Vegeta were in  their base states this didn't bother  anyone as far as I can remember however  if you put mirrors in a one-on-one  situation where Goku is actively trying  a substantial amount mirrors his  abilities seem like much more of a  character contrivance than an actual  character trait and what I mean by that  is due to the absurd power achieved  through tremendous effort throughout the  series Goku and his friends have been  recognized time and time again even by  the gods as being exceptional  individuals for achieving the power they  have for myris to appear with these  abilities without any explanation seems  unreasonable for an audience to  unanimously accept given how power and  speed correlated in Dragon Ball and even  Dragon Ball super  when Mira suggests that he can't stand  against Mauro it comes across as more of  a plot convenience than a believable  thing  happen within the story for this  character especially when you consider  that characters in the past even if they  were primarily quick always had  overwhelming power to correlate even if  you take extreme examples like Dragon  Ball super is dispo whose entire gimmick  was that he was incredibly quick he  still possessed the ability to utilize  that speed in order to outmatch much  stronger opponents like Frieza all in  all it just seems incredibly contrived  that mirus can teach Goku ultra instinct  and outmaneuver Super Saiyan 3 in ways  that people like Wiest can't however the  most egregious and Glary example of bad  writing in this story so far surfaced in  chapter 50 when Mauro revealed his third  wish towards the end of the 48th chapter  three wishes are granted tomorrow and  his companion the first restore  Cranberry to perfect health  the second restore Mauro to his full  power and the third is kept secret  this made Mauro much more interesting to  me not just because of his power but  also because I enjoy the idea of a  villain that held his cards close to his  chest one that would have a great plan  on a cosmic scale and because he had his  ego in check didn't feel the need to  monologue his plan away to the audience  and he rose organically creating tension  while carving out his own unique  identity that's why this cliffhanger  worked so well when it dropped for me  the idea that a villain had made a wish  with the Dragon Balls but didn't let us  know what that wish was is exactly the  kind of surprise I'd live for in  storytelling so much so that I was  surprised something like this hadn't  already been attempted before in Dragon  Ball it's so simple yet so effective I  love this running technique as it's a  wonderful way to build tension the best  example of this I can think of can be  seen with attack on Titans key to the  basement with Aaron's basement key and  Mauro's third wish  the writer is making a promise to us the  audience that whatever is revealed will  be worth it and change everything for  this technique to be successful it needs  to create the aha moment when it's  revealed the viewers must think of  course that makes so much sense now  fueling the story with a freshness only  a satisfying well thought-out reveal can  provide needless to say I was excited to  see what was in store for me the rest of  the chapter went on  by teasing the third wish the entire  next chapter proceeds on by its teased  again as these months pass on by 